U2[21] = {
"q": "Explain the structure of an HRD system and describe its major components.",
"intro": """A system is a set of parts that work together for a purpose. An HRD system is therefore not a training calendar hanging on a wall. It is an organised arrangement of beliefs, mechanisms, people and information that continuously develops individuals, boss–subordinate pairs (dyads), teams, and the organisation as a whole.

The notes insist that HRD is a process, not merely a bag of techniques. Techniques such as performance appraisal, counselling, training and organisation development are used to initiate, facilitate and promote that process. Because the process has no limit, the mechanisms must be examined periodically to see whether they are promoting or hindering development. Structure means how these parts are arranged and linked — like organs of one body, not like separate shops in a market.""",
"pts": [
("Foundation of the structure — philosophy and goals",
"""At the base are beliefs that people have potential and that development is planned and valued. Goals of the HRD system, from the notes, include: capabilities of each employee as an individual; in the present role; in expected future roles; the dyadic relationship with the supervisor; team spirit in every unit; collaboration among units; and the organisation’s overall health and self-renewing capability. If this foundation is missing, later components become disconnected activities that fill files but do not develop anyone."""),
("Performance appraisal as a central component",
"""This is a planned review of results and behaviours. In an HRD structure it is developmental: it clarifies the role, identifies strengths and gaps, and leads to an improvement plan. It is not only a confidential mark for increment. Appraisal sits near the centre because it regularly generates information for other components."""),
("Potential appraisal",
"""This component looks forward: can this person take a larger or different responsibility? It prevents the organisation from promoting people only because they are excellent in the present job (a specialist may fail as a manager). Potential appraisal feeds career planning and succession."""),
("Training and development",
"""Planned learning to close gaps and to prepare for future work. It includes training (job skills), education (concepts and judgement) and longer development. In the structure, training is a component that receives needs from appraisal and TNA and returns people to the workplace where other components must support transfer."""),
("Career planning",
"""A continuous mapping of possible paths from entry toward later roles, integrating individual aspirations with organisational needs. Notes call it a blueprint, a process not an event, and a means of managing people for optimum results. It uses potential data and specifies what training and job experiences are required."""),
("Feedback, counselling, and OD/climate mechanisms",
"""Feedback gives specific information on behaviour and results. Counselling is a helping conversation about problems, emotions and choices so the person can decide and change. Organisation development, team building and climate work develop groups and the whole organisation’s health — which the notes say must also be covered by development. These are the human and cultural components of the structure."""),
("Supporting structure — roles, records and HRM links",
"""HRD specialists design and facilitate; line managers develop people daily; top management sponsors. There must be records (competencies, IDPs, training history). Links to HRM — placement, promotion, pay — must be consistent, otherwise HRD messages are cancelled by reward messages. Structure includes these supports, not only the six famous mechanisms."""),
("Interrelation is itself part of structure",
"""The parts must form a cycle: goals → performance → feedback/counselling → potential and career → training and experiences → new performance. A review loop checks whether mechanisms help or hinder the HRD process. A student who only lists components without saying they are linked has not explained structure."""),
],
"conc": """Define HRD as a system with a philosophy at the base. Describe components: appraisal, potential, training, career, feedback/counselling, OD/climate, plus roles and information. Stress that structure means linkage and a review loop, not a pile of forms. Close: the HRD system develops people at many levels so that the organisation can renew itself."""
}

U2[22] = {
"q": "Explain performance appraisal as an HRD subsystem and state its importance for employee development.",
"intro": """A subsystem is a part of a larger system that has its own work but exists for the system’s purpose. Performance appraisal as an HRD subsystem means a planned, usually periodic, discussion of how well an employee has done, which behaviours helped or hurt, and what learning should follow. This is different from a secret number used only to decide bonus or punishment.

If appraisal does not develop the person, it is not yet an HRD subsystem — it is only an administrative ritual. Many Indian employees say ‘appraisal does not help us grow’. This question asks you to explain the HRD version and why that version matters for development.""",
"pts": [
("Meaning of appraisal in HRD",
"""It is a cycle: understand the role, set goals, review results and competencies against those goals, give and receive feedback, and write an individual development plan (IDP). Both boss and employee prepare. The spirit is ‘how do we improve together’, not ‘how do I catch you’. Self-appraisal is often included so that the employee thinks before the meeting."""),
("Role clarity and goal setting — where development starts",
"""People cannot grow if they do not know what ‘good work’ means. Jointly agreed key result areas and expected behaviours make the later review fair. Unclear jobs make appraisal political. Importance for development: energy is directed, not wasted."""),
("Diagnosis of strengths and gaps",
"""Appraisal produces a map: what to use more, what to train, what to coach, whether the problem is skill or something else. This map is the raw material of HRD. Without it, training is a guess and counselling has no agenda."""),
("Feedback becomes regular, not accidental",
"""Human beings cannot correct what they never hear. A yearly (and preferably more frequent) review makes feedback official. Importance: the employee’s self-image is compared with reality in a structured way, which is the beginning of learning."""),
("The development plan is the heart of importance",
"""Every HRD appraisal should end with two or three concrete actions — a course, a project, mentoring, a job aid — with dates and a person responsible. If there is no IDP, appraisal has stopped at judgement and has not entered development."""),
("It feeds other HRD subsystems",
"""Training needs, career talks, potential reviews and counselling topics come from appraisal. That is why it is often drawn at the centre of an HRD system diagram. Importance is not only the one-hour meeting; it is the information it sends to the rest of HRD."""),
("Motivation and the boss–subordinate relationship",
"""Fair recognition of good work motivates. The discussion also develops the dyad (employee and boss), which Unit 1 listed as an HRD goal. A skilled appraisal conversation is itself a developmental experience: the boss practises coaching; the employee practises reflection."""),
("When appraisal fails as HRD — to show you understand importance",
"""Forced ranking, surprise ratings, no counselling, and no follow-up teach fear. Then appraisal harms development. Importance exists only if design is developmental: trained managers, time protected, climate of trust, and follow-up. Write this caution so the answer is not naive."""),
],
"conc": """Explain appraisal as a developmental cycle (goals, review, feedback, IDP), not as a salary form. Importance: role clarity, diagnosis, feedback, development planning, links to training and career, motivation, and a stronger boss–subordinate bond. Without developmental appraisal, HRD has no regular organisation-wide engine."""
}

U2[23] = {
"q": "Describe training as an HRD subsystem and explain how it contributes to employee development.",
"intro": """Training is a planned organisational effort to help employees learn job-related competencies — knowledge, skills and attitudes needed to perform work. One definition in the notes (Noe and others) is: a planned effort by the company to facilitate employees’ learning of job-related competencies. Another simple idea is that training is offered especially when there is a performance deficiency that is due to lack of skill.

In HRD, training is one subsystem, not the whole of HRD. It contributes to development when it is based on real needs and when the workplace allows the new learning to be used. A picnic with a lecture is not a training subsystem.""",
"pts": [
("Place of training in the HRD system",
"""Training receives its purpose from Training Needs Assessment and from appraisal gaps. It uses design (objectives, methods, resources) and delivery, then evaluation. People return to bosses, climate and career systems that must support transfer. Isolated training is an event; as a subsystem it is a link in a chain."""),
("Contribution through knowledge, skill and attitude",
"""Employee development is not only ‘knowing facts’. A customer officer needs product knowledge, listening skill, and an attitude of respect. Training that only lectures facts under-develops the person. Good training uses methods that match the type of learning: practice for skill, discussion for attitude, explanation for knowledge."""),
("Contribution to present-job effectiveness",
"""When need = standard performance minus actual performance, and the cause is KSA, training raises proficiency, reduces errors and builds confidence. A machine operator trained on a new process produces more with fewer defects. Individual development here is visible in the current role."""),
("Contribution to future roles and change",
"""New technology, laws, quality systems or a promotion require learning before the person fails in public. Combined with education (concepts, judgement), training prepares expected future roles — which is an HRD objective in T.V. Rao’s definition. Development is then preventive, not only remedial."""),
("Morale, satisfaction and employability",
"""Notes list benefits: higher job satisfaction and morale, motivation, lower turnover, better company image, capacity to adopt new methods. Being trained is a signal that the organisation invests in the person. That signal is itself developmental: the employee feels valued and stays to grow further."""),
("Socialisation and culture",
"""Induction training develops understanding of the organisation’s purpose, customers, rules and expected behaviour. New employees become members, not only job holders. This is development of the person-in-the-organisation, not only of a pair of hands."""),
("Limits — so that contribution is not overstated",
"""Training cannot fix a broken machine, an impossible target, or a cruel boss. If climate is weak, contribution dies after the classroom. That is why training must remain a subsystem among appraisal, counselling and career. The notes also warn against off-the-shelf programmes and against treating training as the answer to all problems."""),
("How to maximise its contribution to development",
"""Start with TNA; write clear objectives; choose suitable methods (on-the-job, off-the-job, e-learning); deliver well; involve the boss in transfer; evaluate learning, behaviour and results. Then training truly develops employees instead of entertaining them."""),
],
"conc": """Describe training as planned learning of job competencies and as one HRD subsystem linked to needs and transfer. It contributes by building KSA for present and future work, morale, and socialisation — provided diagnosis is honest and the workplace supports use of learning. Training develops people; a training calendar alone does not."""
}

U2[24] = {
"q": "Explain career planning and potential appraisal as HRD subsystems.",
"intro": """Both of these subsystems look at tomorrow. Performance appraisal mostly asks: how well was the present job done last year? Potential appraisal asks: how far can this person go? Career planning asks: what path shall we follow, together, so that the person’s growth and the organisation’s needs meet? Unit 1 said HRD must develop capabilities for expected future roles. These two subsystems are how that future is managed.

A beginner should not mix them. Potential is a judgement of capacity. Career planning is a process of goals and paths. They work best when they are integrated.""",
"pts": [
("Potential appraisal — meaning",
"""It is a systematic estimate of an employee’s capability to handle higher or different responsibilities. Tools may include reviews by more than one boss, assessment centres (exercises, discussions, in-basket tests), work samples, and sometimes tests. It should be separated from the performance rating so that a brilliant specialist is not automatically labelled a future general manager."""),
("Potential appraisal — purpose in HRD",
"""Identify talent early; invest development money where it will matter; avoid the Peter Principle (promoting people until they fail); give the employee a fair reading of how the organisation sees his or her future. Without potential appraisal, career talk is either flattery or guesswork."""),
("Career planning — meaning",
"""Notes define it as the systematic process by which one selects career goals and the path to those goals. A career plan is a blueprint in which the career is mapped from entry toward later years (not necessarily only to retirement in today’s world of change). It is a process of developing human resources rather than an event. It is not an end in itself but a means of managing people to obtain optimum results. It is continuous because the environment keeps changing."""),
("Whose responsibility",
"""Career planning is primarily the individual’s responsibility, but it is the organisation’s responsibility to provide information, counselling and opportunities, and to integrate employee goals with organisational goals. HRD’s job is this joint process, not forcing a path the person hates, and not ignoring the person either."""),
("Process of career planning (from notes)",
"""Identify individual needs and aspirations; analyse career opportunities in the organisation; identify match and mismatch; formulate and implement strategies (training, rotation, education); review the plan as life and the organisation change. Going through this process develops self-awareness — which is itself HRD."""),
("How the two subsystems join",
"""Potential data make career paths realistic. Career plans tell what experiences a high-potential employee needs next. If potential is labelled but no career action follows, the employee becomes bitter. If career promises are made without potential judgement, the organisation makes promises it cannot keep."""),
("Benefits of treating them as HRD subsystems",
"""Succession (someone ready for key jobs); retention of capable people; better promotions; motivation and morale; more stable workforce; better use of human resources — all listed in career-planning notes. They serve both the person and the firm."""),
("Cautions",
"""Secret potential lists, bias (gender, region, loyalty over ability), and rigid ladders in a fast-changing firm harm HRD. There should be more than one direction (specialist excellence as well as managerial ladder), feedback to the employee, and review. Otherwise these subsystems become politics."""),
],
"conc": """Define potential appraisal as judgement of future capacity and career planning as a continuous joint path-setting process. Show their link, process, benefits and cautions. Together they make HRD long-term rather than a series of short training events."""
}

U2[25] = {
"q": "Explain the role of feedback and counselling in an HRD system.",
"intro": """Forms, ratings and training certificates do not by themselves change a human being. Conversation does. Feedback is information given to a person about his or her behaviour or results, so that the person can continue what is effective and change what is not. Counselling, in the notes, is discussion of an emotional problem with the general objective of decreasing it; it is also described as purposeful conversation in an understanding atmosphere so that the individual can make his or her own decision from among available choices.

In an HRD system, feedback and counselling are the human processes that make appraisal, training and career planning actually work. Without them, HRD is paperwork.""",
"pts": [
("Role of feedback — information for improvement",
"""Good feedback is specific, timely, and about behaviour or work, not about the person’s character. ‘In yesterday’s client meeting you interrupted twice, so we missed their concern’ can be used. ‘You are poor’ cannot. Feedback reduces the gap between how the employee sees himself and how the work really looks. That gap-reduction is learning."""),
("Feedback must be two-way",
"""The employee also feeds back on unclear orders, missing tools, and unrealistic targets. This improves the organisation, not only the individual, and matches the HRD climate value of openness. One-way scolding is not HRD feedback."""),
("Role of counselling — a helping relationship",
"""Counselling deals with an emotional problem through communication, generally to understand and reduce the disorder. Conditions may be on or off the job: job dissatisfaction, resistance to change, alienation, frustration, conflict, stress, aggression, apathy, withdrawal. The counsellor — often the manager, sometimes a specialist — helps the person think more clearly and choose. Basic purpose: assist the individual to decide, not to issue orders in a soft voice."""),
("Functions and aims of counselling (from notes)",
"""Functions: advise, reassure, communicate, release of emotional tension, clarified thinking, reorientation. Aims listed by Eisenberg and Delaney include understanding self, making decisions, setting achievable growth goals, planning present for desired future, solving personal and interpersonal problems, coping, controlling self-defeating emotions, better transaction skills, and positive self-regard. These aims are clearly developmental."""),
("Link to appraisal, training and career",
"""Without a counselling discussion, appraisal is a signature on a form. After training, feedback tells whether behaviour changed. Career counselling prevents wild hopes and maps next steps. Thus feedback and counselling connect every other subsystem to the living person."""),
("Who should counsel, and cautions",
"""Supervisors/managers, specialists, and professional counsellors. Managers need training in listening. Some cases (serious mental health, family crisis) need referral. Confidentiality is essential; leaked counselling destroys HRD climate. The counsellor’s approach depends on the situation and the personality of the counselee."""),
("A simple counselling process (notes)",
"""Step 1: describe the changed behaviour; show concern with work performance. Step 2: get employee comments; keep negative comments on performance, not on character. Step 3: agree on a solution; emphasise confidentiality. Step 4: summarise and get commitment. Step 5: follow up. This process is the role made practical."""),
("Climate role",
"""Skilled, respectful feedback and counselling build trust and openness. Harsh, public, or absent feedback destroys climate. So their role is both individual development and system health. HRD must train managers in these skills; otherwise the whole system is a machine without a heart."""),
],
"conc": """Feedback informs the employee about work in a usable way; counselling helps the person handle emotions, choices and problems so that change can happen. Together they turn HRD data into human development. Write definitions, roles, links, process and confidentiality in the exam."""
}

U2[26] = {
"q": "Describe the major principles for designing effective HRD practices.",
"intro": """A principle is a design rule you follow so that the practice actually develops people. HRD practices mean appraisal schemes, training programmes, career systems, counselling arrangements, OD workshops, and climate-building activities. Many organisations copy a form from another company and call it HRD. Effectiveness then fails. The notes say mechanisms must be examined to see whether they promote or hinder the HRD process. Principles are how you design so that they promote.""",
"pts": [
("Top-management commitment and HRD philosophy",
"""Leaders must believe that people have potential and must fund time and money. If the chief executive cancels every programme when targets are tight, staff learn that HRD is optional. Design starts with a signed policy and visible modelling, not with a form."""),
("Line-manager ownership",
"""HRD is every manager’s job. Practices must be designed so that bosses can use them daily — simple enough, trained enough. If only the HRD officer ‘owns’ appraisal or coaching, the practice dies when that officer is busy. Involve line managers in design."""),
("Integration of subsystems",
"""Appraisal, training, career, potential and counselling must share purpose and data. Designing a beautiful training course that never talks to appraisal violates systems thinking. Effective design is a network, not a showcase piece."""),
("Alignment with organisational strategy",
"""Practices should build the capabilities the organisation’s future needs. A fashionable leadership course is ineffective if strategy needs technicians for a new plant. Principle: start from goals, then design the practice."""),
("Need-based, diagnostic, scientific",
"""Design from TNA, role analysis and climate data, not from a vendor brochure or from copying a neighbour firm. The notes warn against off-the-shelf programmes. Cause-and-effect analysis first: is this even a people-development problem?"""),
("Fit with culture, while building climate",
"""Adapt tools to existing values (hierarchy, language, union history) and slowly build openness and trust. Shock implementation of foreign tools in a fearful climate produces fake data. Principle: respect context; do not confuse ‘best practice elsewhere’ with ‘best practice here’."""),
("Participation, fairness and transparency",
"""Employees should understand criteria and have a voice (self-appraisal, two-way feedback). Equity of opportunity is an HRD objective in the notes. A practice that only develops favourites is not effective HRD even if those few become very skilled."""),
("Continuity and evaluation",
"""HRD has no finish line. Build follow-up, not one-day events. Review whether the practice helps or hinders development; redesign if it has become ritual. Use evaluation (including Kirkpatrick levels for training) and climate surveys. What is not reviewed becomes paperwork."""),
],
"conc": """List principles as headings with a short example each: commitment, line ownership, integration, strategy alignment, diagnosis, culture-climate fit, fairness, and evaluation. Effective HRD practices are designed on these rules; copied forms without principles become rituals."""
}

U2[27] = {
"q": "Explain how the major HRD subsystems are interrelated within an organisation.",
"intro": """Interrelated means the output of one subsystem is the input of another. If a student only lists appraisal, training, career, potential, feedback and counselling, the question is not answered. You must show the arrows. Think of a yearly cycle in a living organisation, not of six separate offices that never speak.""",
"pts": [
("From performance appraisal to counselling and training",
"""Appraisal identifies a gap, for example weak customer listening. That gap becomes the counselling agenda (why is it happening? skill or fear?) and, if it is a skill gap, a training need. If this link is cut, the same person is rated ‘poor communication’ every year and never helped. Development fails."""),
("From training back to appraisal",
"""After training, new behaviours should appear in the next goals and reviews. The boss should look for transfer. This closes the learning loop. If nobody checks, training is a picnic between two appraisals."""),
("From potential appraisal to career planning",
"""High potential suggests an accelerated path, rotation, mentoring. Limited potential for managerial work suggests a specialist excellence path rather than a forced promotion. Career without potential is guesswork; potential without career action is a broken promise."""),
("From career plans to training and job experiences",
"""A career plan specifies what to learn and which jobs to taste. The training calendar should partly come from aggregated career and IDP needs, not only from vendor catalogues. Job rotation is a career tool as well as a training method."""),
("Feedback as connecting tissue",
"""Every subsystem needs conversation: feedback after potential review, after training, during daily work. Feedback is not a seventh isolated box; it is the blood of the system. Without it, files move and people do not."""),
("Climate and OD as the common context",
"""All links fail in a fearful climate because data become fake. Organisation development and climate work therefore interrelate with every subsystem. Trust makes honest appraisal possible; honest appraisal makes useful training possible."""),
("Links with HRM (promotion, pay, placement)",
"""If HRD says ‘collaborate’ but bonus is only for individual targets, employees follow the bonus. Interrelation includes consistency with HRM. Placement after training (using the new skill) is also a link; unused skill dies."""),
("Shared information system",
"""A common competency language and one employee file (history of appraisals, courses, career talks) make interrelation operational. Without memory, each year starts from zero and the process T.V. Rao described cannot be continuous."""),
],
"conc": """Describe a cycle: goals and performance → feedback and counselling → potential and career → training and experiences → new performance, sitting on climate and consistent HRM. Interrelated subsystems create development; silos create activity without growth."""
}

U2[28] = {
"q": "Explain the need for integrating HRD practices with organisational strategy.",
"intro": """Strategy is the organisation’s chosen path to its goals — which products, which customers, what quality, what technology, what cost. HRD practices build the people who must walk that path. Integration means HRD questions are asked in the same breath as strategy questions: if we want X in three years, what must our people be able to do, and how will we develop that?

Need means why this integration is necessary, not merely ‘it is good’. Unconnected HRD can be busy, popular and still useless to the organisation.""",
"pts": [
("Strategy is implemented by people, not by slides",
"""A board decision to digitalise branches, enter a new state, or raise quality will fail if staff cannot use systems, sell the new offer, or lead the new teams. Capability is strategy execution. That is the first need for integration."""),
("Avoid irrelevant and wasteful training",
"""Without strategy, the calendar fills with fashionable topics. Budget and employee time are limited. Integration is needed as a filter: what not to train. Opportunity cost is a real need in poor and mid-sized firms as well as large ones."""),
("Talent must be ready in time",
"""Strategy may need twenty new team leaders in two years. Career and potential systems must start now. You cannot hire overnight always; internal development takes time. Integration is about calendar of people, not only calendar of courses."""),
("Different strategies need different climates",
"""A cost-leadership strategy needs discipline and waste control. An innovation strategy needs psychological safety to try and fail. HRD climate work must match the strategy type. Integration is cultural, not only technical."""),
("Change and resistance",
"""Strategic change frightens people. Integrated HRD (communication, reskilling, counselling) reduces resistance. If HRD is not in the change plan, the strategy remains a circular."""),
("Credibility and survival of the HRD function",
"""When HRD can show contribution to strategic outcomes, it is funded. When it cannot, it is the first budget cut. Integration is needed for HRD’s own effectiveness and status — which then helps employees, because an unfunded HRD cannot develop them."""),
("Meaning and motivation for employees",
"""People learn better when they see why. ‘We are learning this because we are opening rural branches next year’ is more motivating than ‘please attend’. Integration gives meaning, which supports transfer."""),
("What integration looks like in practice (to complete the need)",
"""Competency maps from strategy; TNA against those maps; IDPs reviewed in business meetings; succession for strategic roles; stopping obsolete courses. Need is not abstract — it shows up as these habits."""),
],
"conc": """Integration is needed because people execute strategy, resources are scarce, talent takes time, climate must match strategy, change must be supported, HRD must stay credible, and employees need meaning. Unintegrated HRD is activity without impact on organisational goals."""
}

U2[29] = {
"q": "An organisation has an appraisal system but employees report that it does not help their development. Apply HRD principles to improve the appraisal process.",
"intro": """This is an application question. Do not write a general essay ‘appraisal is important’. The organisation already has a system; employees say it does not develop them. Typical reasons: it is only for increment; ratings are secret or surprising; no counselling; no follow-up; managers are untrained; climate is fearful; no link to training or career. Apply HRD principles (purpose, participation, line skill, integration, climate, continuity, evaluation) to redesign the process.""",
"pts": [
("Clarify purpose in policy and in every meeting",
"""State in writing that the primary purpose of this appraisal is development — understanding performance and planning growth. Rewards may use some of the same data, but the conversation is about improvement. Mixed messages (‘fill this for increment’) are why employees feel it does not help. Principle: developmental purpose."""),
("Begin with role clarity and jointly set goals",
"""If employees never agreed key result areas, the review feels unfair and random. At the start of the period, boss and employee set goals and expected behaviours. Principle: participation and fairness."""),
("Train managers in evidence-based feedback",
"""Replace labels (‘average’, ‘attitude problem’) with incidents and impact. Teach listening. Untrained bosses are a main reason appraisal does not develop anyone. Principle: line managers as developers need skill."""),
("Protect a real counselling conversation",
"""Ban signing forms in the corridor. Give a quiet time slot. Two-way talk: employee’s self-appraisal first. Keep criticism on performance, not character. Confidentiality. Principle: counselling is the heart of HRD appraisal."""),
("End with an individual development plan and follow-up dates",
"""Two or three actions — coaching, a course, a project — with dates, reviewed after a quarter. Without this, the meeting was judgement, not development. Principle: continuity."""),
("Link appraisal to training, career and potential",
"""Gaps go to TNA. Aspirations go to career discussion. High performers with leadership behaviours go to potential review. Employees will start to feel the system helps when something actually happens after the form. Principle: integration."""),
("Climate safeguards",
"""No public humiliation of ratings. Some appeal on process. Fair standards across bosses (moderation). Fearless data is better data. Principle: HRD climate."""),
("Evaluate the appraisal process itself",
"""Once a year ask employees: did this review help you develop? Hold managers accountable for quality of conversations, not only for submitting forms on time. Redesign what hinders the HRD process. Principle: evaluation."""),
],
"conc": """Apply principles as a redesign: purpose, joint goals, trained feedback, counselling time, IDP, links to other subsystems, climate safeguards, and evaluation of appraisal itself. Do not invent a new form colour and call it HRD. Employees will say appraisal helps only when something developmental follows the discussion."""
}

U2[30] = {
"q": "Apply the concept of HRD subsystems to propose a development path for an employee identified as having future leadership potential.",
"intro": """Do not propose only ‘send the person on an MBA’ or ‘promote immediately’. Leadership potential means the person may be able to take larger people and decision responsibilities in future — it is not a certificate that they are already a leader. Apply all major HRD subsystems over two to four years: potential validation, career planning, performance appraisal, training and education, on-the-job stretch, mentoring, feedback and counselling.""",
"pts": [
("Validate potential fairly (potential appraisal subsystem)",
"""Use more than one source: current results, behaviour with people, assessment centre or work sample, views of more than one senior. Wrong labels destroy careers and create politics. Tell the employee honestly what was seen as potential and what is still a risk (for example, impatience)."""),
("Draw a career map together (career planning)",
"""Discuss possible roles in three to five years (team leader, project manager, branch head) and the employee’s own aspirations. Identify competency gaps versus those roles. Career planning notes say individual and organisational goals must be integrated. Write a path with more than one possible fork, not a single promised throne."""),
("Keep present performance strong (performance appraisal)",
"""Potential is not a holiday from the current job. Appraisal should include both results and leadership behaviours: coaching juniors, decision quality, integrity, collaboration. Weak present performance with a ‘high potential’ tag will not be believed by others."""),
("Stretch experiences — the main developer",
"""Job rotation, leading a project, deputy to a manager, cross-functional task force, handling a difficult customer segment. Potential grows mostly through carefully supported difficult work, not through classroom only. Each stretch should have a learning goal."""),
("Training and education mix",
"""Conceptual inputs (how the business makes money, basics of finance, people management) plus skill workshops (feedback, conflict, planning). Time them just before or during stretch roles so that transfer is possible."""),
("Mentor plus counselling",
"""A senior mentor who is not always the direct boss. Counselling for transition stress, self-doubt, or derailers such as arrogance. Notes: counselling helps the person decide and cope. Confidentiality matters; a high-potential label can create loneliness and pressure."""),
("Rich feedback, including 360-degree if climate allows",
"""Leadership is seen by subordinates, peers and customers, not only by the boss. Frequent, specific feedback catches derailers early. If culture is not ready for 360, use structured stakeholder comments privately."""),
("Review, organisational support, honest revision",
"""A talent council or HRD committee reviews progress yearly. Rewards should recognise development effort, not only last month’s sales. If potential was overestimated, revise the path honestly — specialist excellence is also honourable. If progress is good, the next stretch is offered. This is HRD as a process without a false full stop."""),
],
"conc": """Propose a multi-year path that uses potential appraisal, career planning, performance goals, stretch jobs, training, mentoring, feedback and counselling together. Leadership potential becomes leadership capability only through an integrated HRD path, not through a title or a single course."""
}
